#N canvas 100 100 640 520 12;
#X obj 40 20 inlet;
#X obj 40 70 moses 0.001;
#X obj 40 120 t f b;
#X obj 220 120 array size $1;
#X obj 40 180 pack f f;
#X obj 40 230 unpack f f;
#X obj 40 280 expr max(round($f2 / (44100 * 60 / $f1 * 4))\, 1) * (44100 * 60 / $f1 * 4);
#X msg 40 340 \; $1 resize \$1;
#X text 150 20 bpm in (float)\, guarded > 0, f 160;
#X text 150 400 bpm_bar_resize~ $1: NEW (never existed in the Max patch). Snaps array $1's length to a whole number of 4/4 bars at the incoming BPM\, so the buffer length always lines up with bar boundaries for slicing/looping. HARDCODED ASSUMPTIONS (edit here if they ever change): 4 beats/bar (4/4 time only) and 44100 Hz sample rate -- both baked into the [expr] below as literal numbers\, not queried dynamically (kept simple/low-risk since there is no real Pd binary in this sandbox to verify a dynamic samplerate~ query against). Flow: bpm float in -> [moses 0.001] guards against <= 0 (divide-by-zero) -> [t f b] fires a bang first (queries the array's CURRENT length via [array size $1]) then the bpm float second\, both landing on [pack f f] in the right order (bang-derived length -> cold/right inlet first\, bpm -> hot/left inlet last) so pack always outputs one correctly-paired [bpm current_length] list per bpm message -> [unpack f f] splits it back into two floats feeding [expr]'s two inlets ($f1=bpm\, $f2=current_length) -> [expr] computes bar_samples = 44100 * 60 / bpm * 4 then new_length = max(round(current_length / bar_samples)\, 1) * bar_samples -> a semicolon message box sends "; $1 resize <new_length>" straight to the array. Note: because Pd's [expr] recomputes on ANY inlet receiving a value (not just a single hot inlet)\, the very first bpm message of a session can trigger one harmless extra recompute using a not-yet-set internal value before the second (correct) recompute immediately follows and produces the final\, correct resize -- verify this in a real Pd install\, not tested against a live binary here., f 460;
#X connect 0 0 1 0;
#X connect 1 1 2 0;
#X connect 2 1 3 0;
#X connect 3 0 4 1;
#X connect 2 0 4 0;
#X connect 4 0 5 0;
#X connect 5 0 6 0;
#X connect 5 1 6 1;
#X connect 6 0 7 0;
